= Enzyme =

== Overview ==
An '''enzyme''' is a biological catalyst that accelerates a specific chemical reaction without being consumed stoichiometrically in that reaction. Most enzymes are proteins, but some RNA molecules are catalytic. Enzymes make many reactions proceed at biologically useful rates under the conditions inside living systems.

== Catalytic mechanism ==
Enzymes bind substrates or otherwise organize reactants so that a reaction can proceed through a pathway with a lower effective activation barrier. Many protein enzymes contain an '''active site''', a region where substrate binding and catalysis occur. Catalysis can involve molecular orientation, acid–base chemistry, covalent intermediates, electrostatic effects or other mechanisms; enzymes are not simply rigid locks that fit one shape.

== Rates versus equilibrium ==
An enzyme changes reaction '''kinetics'''. It does not alter the reaction's equilibrium constant or the standard Gibbs-energy difference at a fixed temperature. For a reversible reaction, the catalyst accelerates processes in both directions consistently with the same equilibrium state. See [[Related to::Chemical equilibrium|Chemical equilibrium]].

== Context and limits ==
Enzyme activity depends on temperature, pH, substrate concentration, cofactors, inhibitors and the biochemical environment. Some enzymes need bound metal ions or small organic cofactors; some exhibit cooperative or allosteric regulation. A reaction observed in purified laboratory conditions may behave differently inside a cell.

== Example ==
Catalase facilitates the breakdown of hydrogen peroxide into water and oxygen. The reaction can occur without the enzyme, but catalysis makes it much faster under appropriate conditions. This does not imply that catalase drives every peroxide-related process in a living system.

== Evidence ==
A catalytic claim requires an identified reaction, rate evidence and conditions. A predicted sequence annotation or similarity to another protein alone does not establish that a candidate enzyme carries out the same reaction in vivo.

== Photosynthetic carbon fixation ==
A biological application is [[Related to::Photosynthesis|photosynthesis]], where the enzyme ribulose-1,5-bisphosphate carboxylase/oxygenase (RuBisCO) catalyzes carbon fixation in the Calvin–Benson cycle. Its function is one part of a larger network that also requires ATP, reducing power, substrates and cellular regulation. Presence of an enzyme does not by itself establish net organism-level carbon accumulation.

